 - **Context.** The context holds the prompt and every frame, so `--context` (default and at most 8192, the tower attention's key limit) must exceed N. Prompts may take `min(4096, context - N)` rows. At N = 4096 that is 4096 rows; a voice reference takes one row per frame of its clip (339 for a 16 s clip) plus its transcript's tokens.
-- **Memory.** The codec decodes the whole reply in one pass, so its buffers grow by about 1.19 MiB a frame: 1.19 GiB at 1024 frames, 4.75 GiB at 4096. The server's PCM buffer grows by 8 KiB a frame. When they do not fit the memory budget, startup fails with `OutOfDeviceMemory`.
+- **Memory.** The codec holds the whole reply's activations, so its buffers grow by about 1.19 MiB a frame: 1.19 GiB at 1024 frames, 4.75 GiB at 4096. The server's PCM buffer grows by 8 KiB a frame. When they do not fit the memory budget, startup fails with `OutOfDeviceMemory`.
 - **Default.** Without the flag, the limits and buffers stay as they were.
 
 With `--long-form`, each segment may use up to N frames, and a reply may use 6000 frames in all. Three English replies of 686 to 1483 characters, spoken in a reference voice, took 1.44 to 1.56 frames a character, pauses included. So `--long-form-chars` at about N / 2 leaves a segment room to spare. For example, `--max-frames 4096 --long-form --long-form-chars 2048` speaks inputs of up to 2048 characters in one synthesis. `--long-form-chars` keeps its default whatever N is.
 
+## Silence guard
+
+A synthesis can collapse: the model stops speaking and generates near-digital silence until its end of speech or the frame limit. One measured reply did this for 64 s; another stayed silent for 37 s until the frame limit. The model can also stall and then recover: one reply was silent for 7.4 s and then spoke the rest of its text correctly.
+
+So the engine decodes audio while it generates, every 4 frames for a stream and every 16 frames (0.74 s) otherwise. It ends a synthesis only once 10 s of frames in a row are below -60 dBFS (an RMS of 0.001), so a stall that has not recovered by then is treated as a collapse. Shorter stalls are kept as they are: the server does not trim them.
+
+- The audio made so far, including the silent run, is kept. The reply carries `X-Finish-Reason: silence`: a header on a whole reply, a trailer on a stream.
+- Silence that completes 10 s only in the last frames still counts. It outranks both the end of speech and the frame limit, because the reply collapsed however generation stopped.
+- With `--long-form`, a segment ended this way ends the reply, as a clipped one does.
+- **Whole replies decode in chunks of 16 frames,** with the incremental decode that streams use. This was measured on the 48-request default-voice capture against the previous build:
+  - All 44 normal replies were byte-identical (two passes of 22), including the one with the recovering 7.4 s stall. 9 of 9 streams matched.
+  - The reply with the 37 s silence was cut in both passes at 20.8 s, 10 s into its stall. What remained was a byte-identical prefix of the previous build's audio.
+
